# STEP B1 — APEX BACKEND ANALYSIS

## B1.1: Direct Method References

```bash
grep -rn "ChangedClass\.changedMethod(" src/classes/ > step_b1_refs.txt
grep -rn "new ChangedClass(" src/classes/ >> step_b1_refs.txt
```

Request each file found. For each caller read the call site:
- Uses old signature? → Direct impact
- Depends on old return value? → Direct impact
- Depends on old behavior? → Direct impact

Record: exact file path, method name, line number, specific reason.

## B1.2: Argument Field Availability Check

**Run this for every method found in B1.1 that accesses fields on a parameter object.**

### Part A — Direct field accesses in the changed method

For each impacted method, read its body and identify:
- Every parameter it receives (e.g., `methodName(SObject arg, List<SObject> records)`)
- Every field accessed directly on those parameters (e.g., `arg.SomeField__c`, `record.AnotherField__c`)

Collect these as the **direct field set**.

### Part B — Transitive field accesses through called methods

The changed method may call other methods and pass the same parameter object to them. Those called methods may access additional fields on the same object.

For each method call inside the changed method that passes a parameter object:
- Read the called method's body
- Collect every field it accesses on that same object (e.g., if `isEngage(qt)` is called and `qt` is the parameter, read `isEngage()` and collect `qt.ServiceName__c`, `qt.IsMultiProductTechnicalQuote__c`, etc.)
- If that called method also calls further methods with the same object → go one more level, collect those fields too

Combine direct field set + transitive field set = **complete required field set** for this method invocation.

### Part C — Check SOQL at each call site against complete required field set

For each call site that passes an object built from SOQL:

```bash
grep -rn "SELECT.*FROM [SObjectName]" src/classes/
```

Read the SELECT clause. Check every field in the complete required field set:
- Field IS in SELECT → no risk for this field at this call site
- Field is NOT in SELECT → flag as 🔴 Critical field availability impact

This catches the case where a called helper method accesses a field that was never included in the original SOQL — it will be silently null, causing conditions in the helper to evaluate incorrectly with no error thrown.

### Part D — Report

For each missing field found:
- Record: caller file, caller method, line of the call, SOQL location, the missing field name, which called method accesses it
- Risk: 🔴 Critical — silent null, condition never evaluates correctly, wrong behavior with no error

**What this catches:**
`isNewBusiness(qt)` calls `isEngage(qt)`. `isEngage()` accesses `qt.ServiceName__c`. If the SOQL that built `qt` does not include `ServiceName__c`, then `isEngage()` receives null for that field and its condition silently evaluates wrong — even though `isNewBusiness()` itself never directly touches `ServiceName__c`.

## B1.3: Inheritance and Interface

```bash
grep -rn "extends ChangedClass" src/classes/
grep -rn "implements ChangedInterface" src/classes/
```

Request files found. Check if they depend on changed behavior.

## B1.4: Utility Class and Static Method References

```bash
grep -rn "ChangedClass\." src/classes/ | grep -v "test\|Test"
```

Static utility methods are called widely. Trace all callers.

## B1.5: Inner Class References

```bash
grep -rn "OuterClass\.InnerClass" src/classes/
```

## B1.6: SOQL Field Changes (if change type E)

If a field was removed or renamed from a SOQL query:
```bash
grep -rn "\.OldFieldName__c" src/classes/
grep -rn "\.OldFieldName__c" force-app/
```

Request every file accessing that field. Check if it reads from a query result.

## B1.7: Custom Settings and Custom Metadata

If changed class reads from Custom Settings or Custom Metadata:
```bash
grep -rn "CustomSettingName__c.getInstance\|CustomSettingName__c.getValues" src/classes/
grep -rn "CustomMetadataName__mdt" src/classes/
```

If changed class modifies what Custom Settings return or how they're used:
- Find all callers that read the same Custom Setting
- Check if they depend on the value that changed

## B1.8: Factory and Builder Patterns

```bash
grep -rn "ChangedClass.create\|ChangedClass.build\|ChangedClass.newInstance\|ChangedClass.getInstance" src/classes/
```

Also search for factory classes that instantiate `ChangedClass`:
```bash
grep -rn "new ChangedClass\b" src/classes/
```

## B1.9: Recursion Prevention Check

If the changed class modifies a recursion flag or static variable used to prevent trigger re-entry:
```bash
grep -rn "isExecuted\|hasRun\|processed\|recursionFlag\|TriggerHelper\." src/classes/ | grep -i "static"
```

Check if other triggers or handlers read this flag and rely on its value.

## B1.10: Governor Limit Note

If the changed method adds SOQL inside a loop, adds DML inside a loop, or significantly increases query/DML count:
- Note this as a 🔴 Critical governor limit risk
- Identify the calling context (batch size, trigger context)
- Add to test plan as a bulk data test

## B1.11: Async Processing

```bash
grep -rln "implements Database.Batchable" src/classes/ | xargs grep -l "ChangedClass"
grep -rln "implements Schedulable" src/classes/ | xargs grep -l "ChangedClass"
grep -rln "implements Queueable" src/classes/ | xargs grep -l "ChangedClass"
grep -rln "@future" src/classes/ | xargs grep -l "ChangedClass"
```

## B1.12: Platform Events

If changed class publishes or subscribes:
```bash
grep -rln "EventBus.publish" src/classes/ | xargs grep -l "ChangedClass"
grep -rln "__e\b" src/classes/ | xargs grep -l "ChangedClass"
```

## B1.13: Deep Traversal (Recursive)

For each file found in B1.1–B1.12, treat it as a new anchor and repeat the grep searches using that class name. Continue until no new files found. Track visited files to avoid loops.

Save all findings to: `step_b1_apex.txt`

